windows: commit the lookup tables a piece at a time under Wine too

Each emulated thread has a 272 MB reservation for its block lookup tables (128 MB of page
pointers, 128 MB of page memory, 16 MB for L1), committed when a fault says a part is needed.
On Windows 64 KB is committed around the fault. Under Wine everything from the start of the
reservation to the end of the faulting region was committed at once, so in practice all 272 MB
of every thread was committed memory from the thread's first block on.

Committed memory can be read. On macOS a page of anonymous memory that is only ever read still
becomes a real page (there is no shared zero page), and it counts in the process's footprint
until it is decommitted. Arknights: Endfield reads through its writable memory, in address
order, at about 100 MB a second. With some 160 threads that walked through up to 44 GB of
tables that held nearly nothing: the footprint went from 1 GB to 40 GB in seven minutes on a
24 GB Mac, and the machine swapped. The memory log (runtime patch 0034) and vmmap showed it:
tables of threads that had never run more than a few blocks were fully resident, L1 included,
in one contiguous run of addresses that ended in the middle of a table.

Wine now gets the same 64 KB pieces as Windows, aligned and kept inside the reservation. A
thread that fills its tables takes about 4000 more faults to do so. tests/runtime/scan-memory.c
reads all writable memory with 16 idle threads alive: about 5 GB before, about 370 MB after.

diff --git a/Source/Windows/Common/OvercommitTracker.h b/Source/Windows/Common/OvercommitTracker.h
index ba521e8..9befa48 100644
--- a/Source/Windows/Common/OvercommitTracker.h
+++ b/Source/Windows/Common/OvercommitTracker.h
@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@
 #pragma once
 
 #include <FEXCore/Utils/IntervalList.h>
+#include <algorithm>
 #include <thread>
 #include <shared_mutex>
 
@@ -12,7 +13,7 @@ namespace FEX::Windows {
  */
 class OvercommitTracker {
 private:
-  bool IsWine;
+  [[maybe_unused]] bool IsWine;
   FEXCore::IntervalList<uint64_t> OvercommitIntervals;
   std::shared_mutex OvercommitIntervalsMutex;
 
@@ -35,16 +36,14 @@ public:
     auto Query = OvercommitIntervals.Query(FaultAddress);
 
     if (Query.Enclosed) {
-      if (IsWine) {
-        MEMORY_BASIC_INFORMATION Info;
-        NtQueryVirtualMemory(NtCurrentProcess(), reinterpret_cast<void*>(FaultAddress), MemoryBasicInformation, &Info, sizeof(Info), nullptr);
-        const auto CommitSize = reinterpret_cast<SIZE_T>(Info.BaseAddress) + Info.RegionSize - reinterpret_cast<SIZE_T>(Info.AllocationBase);
-        VirtualAlloc(reinterpret_cast<void*>(Info.AllocationBase), CommitSize, MEM_COMMIT, PAGE_READWRITE);
-      } else {
-        static constexpr size_t MaxFaultCommitSize = 1024 * 64;
-        const auto AlignedFaultAddress = reinterpret_cast<void*>(FaultAddress & FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_MASK);
-        VirtualAlloc(AlignedFaultAddress, std::min(Query.Size, MaxFaultCommitSize), MEM_COMMIT, PAGE_READWRITE);
-      }
+      // FineWine: commit a small piece around the fault, under Wine as on Windows. Committing the
+      // whole reservation makes all of it readable, and on macOS a page that is only ever read
+      // still costs memory (there is no shared zero page): a program that reads through its own
+      // committed memory then turns each thread's 272 MB of lookup tables into real memory.
+      static constexpr uint64_t FaultCommitSize = 1024 * 64;
+      const uint64_t Start = std::max<uint64_t>(FaultAddress & ~(FaultCommitSize - 1), Query.Interval.Offset);
+      const uint64_t End = std::min<uint64_t>(Start + FaultCommitSize, Query.Interval.End);
+      VirtualAlloc(reinterpret_cast<void*>(Start), End - Start, MEM_COMMIT, PAGE_READWRITE);
       return true;
     }
     return false;
